    {
      "id": "normal",
      "kind": "normal",
      "title": "Normal: how factor activity is measured",
      "start": 20,
      "end": 60,
      "altText": "A one-stage clotting assay: patient plasma diluted into FVIII-deficient plasma, then an APTT reagent — the faster it clots, the more FVIII. Clot time is read off a calibration curve and reported in IU/dL (100 IU/dL ≈ 100%). A chromogenic assay is shown beside it: FVIII activity drives FIXa to activate FX, which cleaves a colour substrate — more colour, more FVIII.",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": "n1",
          "start": 20,
          "end": 30,
          "text": "Factor eight activity is usually measured with a one-stage clotting assay. Patient plasma is mixed into plasma that lacks factor eight, and an APTT is run."
        },
        {
          "id": "n2",
          "start": 30,
          "end": 40,
          "text": "The more factor eight the patient has, the faster that mixture clots. A calibration curve turns clotting time into units per decilitre — a hundred is about normal."
        },
        {
          "id": "n3",
          "start": 40,
          "end": 50,
          "text": "A chromogenic assay measures it differently: factor eight helps factor nine-a activate factor ten, and factor ten-a cuts a colour-producing substrate."
        },
        {
          "id": "n4",
          "start": 50,
          "end": 60,
          "text": "More colour means more factor eight. Two methods, one target — and that difference will matter."
        }
      ],
