    {
      "id": "change",
      "kind": "change",
      "title": "What changes: severity and inhibitors",
      "start": 60,
      "end": 104,
      "altText": "A severity bar: severe < 1 IU/dL (frequent spontaneous joint/muscle bleeds), moderate 1–5 (occasional spontaneous; severe with trauma/surgery), mild > 5–40 (bleeds with major trauma or surgery). Then inhibitors: neutralising antibodies against infused FVIII — the most serious complication of replacement therapy; SIPPET found plasma-derived FVIII with vWF gave fewer inhibitors than recombinant FVIII in previously untreated patients.",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": "c1",
          "start": 60,
          "end": 71,
          "text": "Hemophilia severity is set by the baseline factor level. Below one unit per decilitre is severe — frequent spontaneous bleeding into joints and muscles."
        },
        {
          "id": "c2",
          "start": 71,
          "end": 82,
          "text": "One to five is moderate: occasional spontaneous bleeds. Above five up to forty is mild — bleeding mainly with major trauma or surgery."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3",
          "start": 82,
          "end": 93,
          "text": "The most serious complication of treatment is an inhibitor: antibodies that neutralise the infused factor eight."
        },
        {
          "id": "c4",
          "start": 93,
          "end": 104,
          "text": "The SIPPET trial showed that plasma-derived factor eight containing von Willebrand factor caused fewer inhibitors than recombinant factor eight in previously untreated children."
        }
      ],
