    {
      "id": "lab",
      "kind": "lab",
      "title": "In the lab: the Bethesda assay and emicizumab",
      "start": 104,
      "end": 144,
      "altText": "Nijmegen–Bethesda assay: heat-inactivated patient plasma is mixed 1+1 with (buffered) normal pooled plasma and incubated; residual FVIII is compared with a control mix. One Bethesda unit per mL inhibits FVIII by 50% (two BU, 75%). Only residual activities between 25% and 75% are used; stronger inhibitors are diluted. Emicizumab card: it mimics FVIII by bridging FIXa and FX — it shortens the APTT and falsely raises APTT-based one-stage FVIII; it cannot be heat-inactivated; bovine-reagent chromogenic FVIII assays don’t detect it, so they measure true FVIII and work in the Bethesda assay.",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": "l1",
          "start": 104,
          "end": 114,
          "text": "The Nijmegen–Bethesda assay measures an inhibitor. Heat-treated patient plasma is mixed one-to-one with normal pooled plasma and incubated; then the remaining factor eight is compared with a control."
        },
        {
          "id": "l2",
          "start": 114,
          "end": 124,
          "text": "One Bethesda unit per millilitre destroys half the factor eight; two units, three-quarters. Only results between twenty-five and seventy-five percent remaining are used — stronger inhibitors are diluted first."
        },
        {
          "id": "l3",
          "start": 124,
          "end": 134,
          "text": "Emicizumab mimics factor eight by bridging factors nine-a and ten. It shortens the APTT and falsely raises APTT-based factor eight results — and it can’t be removed by heating."
        },
        {
          "id": "l4",
          "start": 134,
          "end": 144,
          "text": "The fix: a chromogenic factor eight assay built on bovine reagents. Emicizumab only binds human factors, so the bovine assay measures true factor eight — and works inside the Bethesda assay too."
        }
      ],
