  "explanation": {
    "what": "Plain language: the lab measures how much clotting factor a patient has, whether antibodies are destroying it, and how new drugs can fool the tests. Scientific terms: one-stage clotting assay (APTT-based) and chromogenic substrate assay; severity — severe < 1 IU/dL, moderate 1–5, mild > 5–40; Nijmegen–Bethesda assay — 1 BU/mL inhibits FVIII by 50% (2 BU by 75%), valid residual activity 25–75%.",
    "why": "Inhibitors neutralise infused FVIII, the most serious complication of replacement. Emicizumab bridges FIXa and FX, shortening APTT-based tests, and is not heat-labile; bovine-reagent chromogenic assays do not detect it because it binds only human FIXa/FX.",
    "measure": "Heat-inactivated patient plasma (and dilutions) mixed 1+1 with buffered pooled plasma, incubated, residual FVIII vs control; dilute samples with > 2 BU/mL. In emicizumab patients, use bovine chromogenic FVIII for monitoring and inside the Bethesda assay.",
    "differs": "One-stage (APTT-based, emicizumab-sensitive) vs chromogenic (bovine: emicizumab-insensitive). Severe vs moderate vs mild by factor level.",
    "examAngle": "Expect: severity cut-offs, Bethesda calculation and valid range, emicizumab assay choice, inhibitor risk by product."
  },
  "comparisons": [
    {
      "id": "osa-vs-csa",
